          %h1#topological_data_analysis Topological data analysis
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#StrategyOfPersistentHomology"} Strategy of persistent topology/homotopy
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#StrategyOfPersistentCohomotopy"} Strategy of persistent cohomology/cohomotopy
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#general"} General
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#implementation"} Implementation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#applications"} Applications
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_quantum_computation"} Relation to quantum computation
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#CohomotopyInTopologicalDataAnalysisReferences"} Cohomotopy in topological data analysis
          %h2#idea Idea
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -60px 0px 0px 20px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/CoCyclesInDataSets-220522.jpg", :width => "420px"}/
          %p
            %em Topological data analysis
            (TDA) is
            %em qualitative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+analysis"} data analysis
            with tools from
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            %em robust
            against uncertainties and noise.
          %p
            This notably includes tools from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology"} ordinary
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"}> co-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homology"}> homology
            \-theory, which in the guise of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
            has become the signature method in TDA; but it also includes more general tools of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+topology"} differential topology
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy+theory"} persistent homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+cohomotopy+theory"} persistent cohomotopy theory
            = succeed "(graphics" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;\;\;\;\;
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/New+Foundations+for+TDA+--+Cohomotopy"} SS22
          %p
            Typically, TDA deals with
            %em large
            data sets modeled as (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsets"} subsets
            of)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            %em data points
            appear as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
            in the topological space of data, and
            %em values
            of data appear as
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
          %h3#StrategyOfPersistentHomology Strategy of persistent topology/homotopy
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -60px 0px 0px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/SomewhatPersistentCycle-220523.gif", :width => "420px"}/
          %p
            The strategy of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology"} persistent homology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} homotopy
            in TDA is to see which chunks (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subspaces"}> subspaces
            ) of the data appear to be (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-connected+topological+space"}> higher
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            when viewed at some resolution (technically: at some
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+topological+space"} filter stage
            %em persist
            as the resolution changes. This may be recorded in
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagrams"} persistence diagrams
            also known as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/barcodes"}> barcodes
            ”.
          %p
            The fundamental theorem of the field – the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stability+of+persistence+diagrams"} stability theorem
            – says that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistence+diagram"} persistent
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"}> co-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycle"}> cycle
            \-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} classes
            are indeed a good invariant of data, in that they remain stable under small perturbations of the initial data (e.g. under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noise"} noise
          %p
            The idea then is that those (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycle"}> co-
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cycles"} cycles
            which persist for longer (appear as longer bars in the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/barcode"} barcode
          %p
            However, it is often unclear (and certainly not part of the mathematical theory) what significance or
            %em meaning
            any persistent cycle has for the practical problem of interpreting data.
            = succeed "(graphics" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;\;\;\;\;\;
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/New+Foundations+for+TDA+--+Cohomotopy"} SS22
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#StrategyOfPersistentCohomotopy Strategy of persistent cohomology/cohomotopy
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 0px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/IndicatorFunctionOnData-220523.jpg", :width => "420px"}/
          %p
            In contrast,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+cohomotopy"} persistent cohomotopy
            in TDA is the effective answer to a concrete and common question in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/data+analysis"} data analysis
          %p
            Given a large-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+CW-complex"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} space
            of data, and a small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+number"} number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"}> real
            ) indicator values assigned to each data point with given precision
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1/r
            %em
              does
              %em any
              data meet a prescribed target indication precisely?
          %p#FundamentalTheoremOfPersistentCohomotopy
            A
            %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#GuaranteeThatSolutionsExist"} fundamental theorem
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+Cohomotopy"} persistent Cohomotopy
            (
            %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcalWagner18"}> Franek, Krčál &amp; Wagner 2018
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcal17"} Franek &amp; Krčál 2017, p. 5
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#GuaranteeThatSolutionsExist"} here
            = succeed "-class" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cohomotopy"} Cohomotopy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computability"} computable
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decidability"} decidable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mphantom
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{--}
            (graphics from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/New+Foundations+for+TDA+--+Cohomotopy"} SS22
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/PersistentCohomotopy-TheIdea-220524b.gif", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (graphics from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/New+Foundations+for+TDA+--+Cohomotopy"} SS22
          %p
            (Alternatively, with tools from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homology+theory"} persistent homology theory
            an answer to this question is given by the method of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well+groups"} well groups
            – but (1.) it is known that well groups are in general too coarse to provide a complete answer and (2.) despite effort it remains unknown if well groups are actually
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computability"} computable
            in relevant cases, see
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcal16"} Franek &amp; Krčál 2016
          %p
            If the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            of data may be assumed to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            (indeed, in typical examples
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is itself a large-dimensional
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin%27s+theorem"} Pontryagin's theorem
            as characterizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/iso-hypersurfaces"} iso-hypersurfaces
            of data (close to a given target indicator) by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/framed+cobordism"} framed
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+theory"} cobordism theory
            (
            %a{:href => "persistent+cohomotopy#FranekKrcal17"}> Franek &amp; Krčál 2017, p. 8-9
            ). The full implications of this relation for topological data analysis remain to be explored.
